<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">La Corona</h1><p class="st-meta">500 CE – 800 CE (approx.) · capital La Corona</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Maya kingdom in north-western Petén, the long-sought 'Site Q', whose panels illuminate its close ties with the Kingdom of Kaan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>For decades looted carved panels from an unknown 'Site Q' circulated on the art market. In 2005 the site was identified as La Corona, a modest centre in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peten">Petén</a> whose rulers used the title of Sak Nikte'. Its dynasty is attested from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-classic-period">Early Classic</a>, around 500 CE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>La Corona lay on a route between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kaan">Calakmul</a> and the south, and its kings were loyal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the Kingdom of Kaan, receiving Kaan princesses as royal brides. Its panels record journeys of local lords to Calakmul and ballgame rituals. In 2012 archaeologists found a stairway block that mentions the bak'tun ending of 2012 in a text about a visit by the Kaan king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuknoom-yichaak-kahk">Yuknoom Yich'aak K'ahk'</a> in 696.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>With Kaan's decline in the 8th century, La Corona's power waned, and it was abandoned around 800 CE. It shows how the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kaan">Snake Kingdom</a> held its network together through marriage and patronage.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/la-corona.jpg" alt="La Corona" width="320" height="195"><figcaption>Image: Adrian Hernandez · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Corona_Relieve_Juego_de_Pelota.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 CE – 800 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>La Corona</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Chak Ak'aach Yuk</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maya religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Classic Maya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sak Nikte'</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=650&amp;focus=la-corona" aria-label="La Corona on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2225 1527 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where La Corona lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2545 1783L2564 1762"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2564 1762h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2545 1783h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2545 1783h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=650&amp;focus=la-corona">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

